#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376582 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "76e86899-2abb-5cca-9585-4a97e4630264" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Differential Equation Governing a Forced Harmonic Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "013bf829-6d33-5fcc-8bba-b99d51c60713" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+3y=15\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376584} {:answer/id #uuid "52d59049-6070-521d-8696-293de1bec4ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+4y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376585} {:answer/id #uuid "7977262c-2a7d-571a-bfa7-5236fa821be0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+3y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376586} {:answer/id #uuid "f0bf89ae-11f6-5131-b017-01c2bd68e13e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+2y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376587} {:answer/id #uuid "9201ed99-6036-5d7e-83ab-140ac9a3d798" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+2y=15\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376588}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52d59049-6070-521d-8696-293de1bec4ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+4y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376585} :answer-field/id #uuid "73e122ad-de35-50db-9d5a-999f0e97c8a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376583}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0f27aa5d-1895-501d-94c1-b1e7c835ede1" :question/math-academy-id "q-64600" :question/problem "A particle of mass $3\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=12\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$  The particle is also subject to the resistive force that's proportional to the particle's velocity with a damping coefficient $c=9\\,\\text{kg/s},$ and an external force given by $F(t)=15\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,\\text{N}.$   \n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of forced harmonic motion with damping is\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-c{y}^{′}-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k,$ $c$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n3{y}^{″}=-9{y}^{′}-12y+15\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+4y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376589 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5266e491-2718-56a9-b5a7-f4a86a8a01c7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Position of a Particle Oscillating With Forced Harmonic Motion at a Moment"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91c780e1-ae5f-5238-81e6-aea0e0462c10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(\\pi )=(2{e}^{-3\\pi }-3)\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376591} {:answer/id #uuid "9e9b10be-246b-5a09-a794-829084d36f68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(\\pi )={e}^{-\\pi }(3{e}^{-2\\pi }-2)\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376592} {:answer/id #uuid "1adf96a0-5d6d-540d-a81e-ebbca61f89fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(\\pi )=2{e}^{-\\pi }({e}^{-2\\pi }-1)\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376593} {:answer/id #uuid "c474055e-f2dd-576f-a44c-e0046802f78d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(\\pi )={e}^{-\\pi }(4{e}^{-2\\pi }-1)\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376594} {:answer/id #uuid "c4008f26-87f9-57e4-ab37-cda241f60295" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(\\pi )=2({e}^{-3\\pi }-4)\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376595}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1adf96a0-5d6d-540d-a81e-ebbca61f89fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(\\pi )=2{e}^{-\\pi }({e}^{-2\\pi }-1)\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376593} :answer-field/id #uuid "d02bcfdd-4a16-5c27-b0d6-8fa4cc1b1524" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376590}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "79553835-e65f-59e1-aba2-8e979bcacb32" :question/math-academy-id "q-64645" :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with forced harmonic motion with light damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+17y=40{e}^{-3t},\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with zero velocity, find the position of the particle after $\\pi $ second." :question/worked-solution "The general solution to the equation of forced harmonic motion with light damping is the sum of the complementary function and a particular solution.\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+17y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +17 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2}+16 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2} & =-16 \\\\ \\lambda +1 & =\\pm 4\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-1\\pm 4\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}={e}^{-t}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t).\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ and $Q$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t).$\n\nThe external force is an exponential force. The constant factor in the exponent is $-2$, which is not a root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular exponential solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{-3t},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined. \n\nSubstituting ${y}_{p}$ into the differential equation and equating coefficients gives $\\alpha =2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=2{e}^{-3t},\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-t}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)+2{e}^{-3t}.\n$$\n\nSince the particle is initially at the origin with zero velocity, the initial conditions are $y(0)=0$ and ${y}^{′}(0)=0.$\n\nSolving with these conditions for $P$ and $Q$ gives\n\n$$\nP=-2,\\,Q=1.\n$$\n\nSo our solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)={e}^{-t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t)+2{e}^{-3t}.\n$$\n\nTo find the position of the particle after $\\pi $ seconds, we substitute into the equation $t=\\pi .$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\pi ) & ={e}^{-\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4(\\pi )-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡4(\\pi ))+2{e}^{-3\\pi } \\\\  & =2{e}^{-\\pi }({e}^{-2\\pi }-1)\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376596 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d29e7ead-5eae-5769-9502-7953d7018782" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations that Represent Forced Harmonic Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8dd066cf-7eb7-59dc-bb41-a2f5256d6ce5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+4y={e}^{5t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376598} {:answer/id #uuid "a2d4c358-334b-51bf-bec6-6a5259d1b854" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}-25y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376599} {:answer/id #uuid "7dbf490c-d109-5c08-b66b-12ae85b46f7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+36y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376600} {:answer/id #uuid "303e30dd-b69e-53a9-9d0d-258f01aa377c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+\\pi {y}^{′}+2y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376601} {:answer/id #uuid "b2a087c2-9112-5b94-847c-670d9810cb0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+7y={e}^{-4t}+t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376602}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2a087c2-9112-5b94-847c-670d9810cb0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+7y={e}^{-4t}+t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376602} :answer-field/id #uuid "a015714c-c91d-5147-9e40-91e18340795f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376597}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c2c5477c-8ea5-5f3e-8509-189eb35b8466" :question/math-academy-id "q-94231" :question/problem "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t>0.$ Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing forced harmonic motion without damping?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t).\n$$\n\nWe can see that the only equation that represents forced harmonic motion without damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+7y={e}^{-4t}+t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376603 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "76e86899-2abb-5cca-9585-4a97e4630264" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Differential Equation Governing a Forced Harmonic Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d5c7d27-eefb-5ad5-b5a7-3b2cd1d5b8e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}+5y=\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376605} {:answer/id #uuid "63ced7a7-284f-5b82-b18a-3621942228ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+\\frac{5}{2}y=\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376606} {:answer/id #uuid "fab92e09-6d90-5451-acc1-ecc8a417570d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+2y=4{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376607} {:answer/id #uuid "1ca71f6e-9d97-5127-a4ea-0f2947efdffa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+4y=2{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376608} {:answer/id #uuid "c2113a69-96dc-55aa-88e2-bf4b065207d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+\\frac{5}{4}y=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376609}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2113a69-96dc-55aa-88e2-bf4b065207d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+\\frac{5}{4}y=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376609} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd4349e2-a0d1-5188-93fd-91e37f8b9c7c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376604}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "06c834d3-f655-589c-886c-ecaa57db3e34" :question/math-academy-id "q-94233" :question/problem "A particle of mass $8\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=10\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$  The particle is also subject to the resistive force that's proportional to the particle's velocity with a damping coefficient $c=16\\,\\text{kg/s},$ and an external force given by $F(t)=4{t}^{2}\\,\\text{N}.$   \n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of forced harmonic motion with damping is\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-c{y}^{′}-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k,$ $c$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n8{y}^{″}=-16{y}^{′}-10y+4{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+\\frac{5}{4}y=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376610 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "813ec1d4-93ec-5a9e-996f-b2e672714927" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Forced Harmonic Motion Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc86b80a-8b5d-5519-8259-9d636519c27d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376612} {:answer/id #uuid "d1a62164-6cf3-502b-8661-5afa9e5a6b22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376613} {:answer/id #uuid "851de8ad-103b-5e46-a2f2-7e2457bf773e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376614} {:answer/id #uuid "4d483b17-6630-52d2-85b7-8f7a08611b9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376615} {:answer/id #uuid "836cdf4c-fbda-5284-8900-fdfb6624cc00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376616}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d483b17-6630-52d2-85b7-8f7a08611b9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376615} :answer-field/id #uuid "7111d041-05c2-50e2-bd88-a7a14f0cc3ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376611}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ec54a4bc-6f62-5d4e-afc1-aa3f503d9658" :question/math-academy-id "q-94382" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+25y=24\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(25t+ϕ)+12\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t+ϕ)+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R$ and $ϕ$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "The general solution to this equation is the sum of the complementary function and a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(t),$ which is the solution to the simple harmonic motion equation ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.$ It can be written in three different forms:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=25\\,⟹\\,\\omega =5.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t$\n\nNext, we find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t).$ The particular solution is of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution into the differential equation and equating coefficients, we find that $\\alpha =1$ and $\\beta =0.$ So, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t+ϕ)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t+ϕ)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\nOnly III matches the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}]}
